## Setup

<!-- TODO(creator): when the design ships no kit.yaml (a private definition whose kit
     lives in the catalog repo), delete this whole catalog section, every link to
     kit.yaml — here and under Files — and promote "Without the catalog" to be Setup. -->
This repository is its own **Starter Kit** ([`kit.yaml`](kit.yaml)), so the normal way to
bring up a new {{AGENT_NAME}} is to pick it from the platform's catalog:

1. **Find it under Starter kits** and open its setup page.
2. **Grant the connections it asks for** — the required ones are checked before anything
   is created:
   <!-- TODO(creator): list connections (GitHub, Slack, Telegram, …), which are required
        vs. suggested, and why each. Keep in step with kit.yaml. -->
3. **Name the agent and create it.** The platform seeds this definition into `$HOME` at a
   pinned commit, creates the schedules, and opens the first session on
   [`ONBOARDING.md`](ONBOARDING.md).
4. **Answer the onboarding dialog.** The agent asks only for what you alone can supply and
   ticks each item off its checklist. Its schedules are **held** until it reports itself
   set up, so nothing runs half-configured.

<!-- TODO(creator): keep this paragraph only if the repo is public — a kit's repository
     must be, since catalogs are read anonymously. -->
Not in a catalog yet? One entry in the catalog repository's `catalog.yaml` adds it, and
the platform's refresh job picks it up without a redeploy:

```yaml
kits:
  - url: https://github.com/<your-org>/{{AGENT_NAME}}
```

### Without the catalog

Create the agent on the platform by hand, grant the same connections, set the environment
variables from the table below, then send it its first message — linking
[`ONBOARDING.md`](ONBOARDING.md) **from the repo (or fork) you actually deploy from**,
since the agent derives its definition repo from that URL:

> Here is a file — read it and set yourself up according to it:
> `https://github.com/<your-org>/{{AGENT_NAME}}/blob/main/ONBOARDING.md`

The runbook handles both paths: it checks out the definition if the platform has not, wires
up `work/`, walks you through the configuration dialog, creates any schedule it cannot
find, and marks itself onboarded.

